Hi,
 I run Gentoo linux on a Celeron-D 325 (2.53 Ghz). I compiled the kernel
with cpufreq, the performance and ondemand governors and p4-clockmod. As
long as I use the performance governor there seems to be no problem (but is
there even a difference with not having cpufreq at all?) I don't run any
ACPI related daemons, or other userspace utilities to interface with
cpufreq, besides cpufreq-info and cpufreq-set.
 According to intel's datasheets, my processor should offer 8 frequency
steps, and this is confirmed by cpufreq-info:
 cpufrequtils 0.3: cpufreq-info (C) Dominik Brodowski 2004
Report errors and bugs to <xxxxxxxxx>, please.
analyzing CPU 0:
driver: p4-clockmod
CPUs which need to switch frequency at the same time: 0
hardware limits: 317 MHz - 2.53 GHz
available frequency steps: 317 MHz, 633 MHz, 950 MHz, 1.27 GHz, 1.58 GHz,
1.90 GHz, 2.22 GHz, 2.53 GHz
available cpufreq governors: ondemand, performance
current policy: frequency should be within 317 MHz and 2.53 GHz.
The governor "performance" may decide which speed to use
within this range.
current CPU frequency is 2.53 GHz (asserted by call to hardware).
 When I switch to the ondemand governor, the cpu frequency goes all the way
down to 317Mhz, in a single step (according to "time_in_state" and
"total_trans" in /sys). The system then runs fine for a short while (under =
0
load), but as soon as the load triggers it to jump to max, the system seems
to crash. That is, my ssh connection fails and I am unable to login again.
 I'm not sure how to check what exactly goes wrong, but if you have
pointers, I can check. For now, I will just attach some general info on the
system.
